 ### [Bounding Box Raymarching](2071-bounding-box-raymarching.md)
-A GPU-based technique that renders a 3D voxel grid by casting rays through a bounding cube using a two-pass approach. It differs by computing ray endpoints directly from the rasterized front and back faces of the bounding box geometry in the fragment shaders, rather than storing pre-computed ray data in a dedicated texture.
+A GPU-accelerated technique that renders a 3D voxel grid by casting rays through the volume's bounding box geometry. It solves the problem of efficiently determining ray entry and exit points for volume traversal without performing expensive ray-cube intersection calculations in the final rendering pass. It differs from Back-face rasterized volume rendering by explicitly utilizing a two-pass approach where the first pass captures back-face coordinates into a texture, and the second pass uses these coordinates to define ray endpoints for front-to-back compositing. Unlike [Volume Ray Casting with RayData Texture](3077-volume-ray-casting-with-raydata-texture.md), which stores ray data in a dedicated texture, this method often computes ray directions from geometry attributes or camera matrices directly in the fragment shader.
